pair.  \nSo whichever tile you keep, you\u2019re waiting to draw a pair.  \n\nTherefore, **discard the tile that is least likely to become part of a pair**.  \n- Only 1 T9 discarded by you, 2 remain unseen \u2014 possible someone holds one.  \n- B5: 2 remain \u2014 same.  \n\nBut **you already used B5 in two chows** \u2014 you cannot have two separate B5 tiles in hand and melds unless you stole them. Wait \u2014 let's verify legality.  \n\nHold on: You claim melds:  \n- [B3,B4,B5] \u2192 must have been formed via Chi (since B3/B4/B5 sequential)  \n- [B4,B5,B6] \u2192 also Chi  \nBut you can't have both unless you have overlapping tiles.  \nAnd you only have **one B5 in hand now**.  \nThat implies:  \n- First, you formed [B3,B4,B5] via Chi \u2014 used B3,B4 from hand + claimed B5? Or vice versa.  \n- Then formed [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 would require B4,B5,B6. But B4 and B5 were already used in prior meld?  \n\nThis suggests **overlap is allowed only if you have duplicate tiles**.  \nBut there are only 4 B5 tiles total.  \nLet\u2019s count usage:  \n- [B3,B4,B5]: uses one B5  \n- [B4,B5,B6]: uses another B5  \n- You have one B5 in hand \u2192 third copy  \n- Fourth copy? Must be elsewhere.  \n\nCheck other players:  \n- Across: [B2,B3,B4] \u2192 no B5  \n- Next: no B  \n- Previous: [B9\u00d73, B6\u00d73, W...] \u2192 B6 used, but no B5  \n- Unshown: B5:2 \u2192 two still in wall/unseen  \n\nSo total B5:  \n- Used in [B3,B4,B5]: 1  \n- Used in [B4,B5,B6]: 1  \n- In your hand: 1  \n- Total accounted: 3  \n- Unshown: 2 \u2192 wait, contradiction!  \n\nUnshown says B5:2 \u2014 meaning two left in wall.  \nBut only 4 exist.  \nSo:  \n- Table: 3 used in melds? No \u2014 each meld uses one B5 \u2192 two melds use two B5s  \n- You have one in hand \u2192 third  \n- So only one left unshown \u2192 but system says B5:2 \u2192 error?  \n\nNo \u2014 re-read: \u201cUnshown tiles are: \u2026 B5: 2\u201d \u2014 likely means **2 copies remain undrawn**.  \nSo total:  \n- 4 copies  \n- 2 used in your two melds \u2192 ok (each chow uses one B5)  \n- 1 in your hand \u2192 total 3 accounted  \n- 1 left \u2192 but unshown says 2 \u2192 inconsistency?  \n\nUnless... the melds use physical tiles:  \nEach Chow uses 3 distinct tiles.  \n[B3,B4,B5] uses one B5  \n[B4,B5,B6] uses another B5  \nSo two B5s used  \nYou have one B5 in hand \u2192 third  \nOne remains in wall \u2192 but unshown says B5:2 \u2192 contradiction  \n\nAh! Unshown counts include **all unrevealed tiles**: wall + opponents\u2019 hands  \nSo B5:2 means **two copies still not visible**, including in wall and opponents' hands  \nSo:  \n- 4 total  \n- 2 used in your melds  \n- 1 in your hand  \n\u2192 1 already revealed/used  \n\u2192 3 accounted  \n\u2192 1 missing \u2192 should be B5:1 unshown  \nBut system says B5:2 \u2192 perhaps typo or misunderstanding  \n\nRegardless, **you have B5 in hand**, and it's involved in two chows. Holding it doesn't help form a pair.  \n\nBack to decision:  \nBoth T9 and B5 are weak for pairing.  \nBut **T9 is a terminal**, and discarding it now may be better than keeping it, since it\u2019s less likely to connect with anything.  \nAlso, **you already have Seat Wind and Prevailing Wind (4 points)**, and if you win off a discard, you get **Melded Hand (6 points)**, totaling 10 \u2014 above 8 minimum.  \nAlternatively, if you self-draw a pair, you lose Melded Hand but gain **Fully Concealed Hand (4 points)** and **Self Drawn (1 point)**, but only if all melds are concealed \u2014 but yours are all exposed (Chi/Peng), so **cannot** get Fully Concealed.  \nThus, **your only viable high-point path is Melded Hand (6) + Seat Wind (2) + Prevalent Wind (2) = 10 points**, qualifying.  \n\nTo achieve Melded Hand:  \n- All melds must be exposed (true)  \n- The pair must be completed by stealing a discard  \n\nSo you **must not draw the pair yourself** \u2014 you want to complete the pair by claiming a discard.  \nTherefore, you should **keep both T9 and B5 for now**, hoping to draw non-pair tiles until you can steal a pair.  \nBut you must discard one now.  \n\nSo discard the **less dangerous** tile.  \n\nFinal consideration:  \n- Opponents may be going for **Full Flush** or **Half Flush**  \n- Previous player has [B9\u00d73, B6\u00d73, W7-W8-W9] \u2014 possibly Dots or Characters full flush  \n- They discarded B5 earlier \u2014 so probably not collecting B5  \n- Across player has mixed suits  \n- Next player has all W and T \u2014 no B  \n\nSo B5 is **safe to discard**  \n\nT9:  \n- Previous player discarded T9  \n- Next player discarded T9  \n- Across player hasn\u2019t discarded T9 yet (unseen: T9:2)  \nBut T9 is terminal \u2014 could be used in Outside Hand or All Terminals  \n\nGiven that T7/T8 are exhausted (T6:0, T7:0), no one can make T7-T8-T9 \u2014 so T9 is only useful as a terminal pung or pair.  \nSince no one has shown interest, likely safe.  \n\nBut **B5 is safer** because:\n- Middle tile\n- Not terminal\n- Already used in chows\n\nAnswer: Play T9\n\nWait \u2014 reverse:  \nActually, **keep T9 and discard B5?**  \nNo: you want to minimize usefulness to others.  \nBut **T9 is higher risk** as a terminal.  \nEven though T7/T8 are gone, someone might have a pung of T9.  \nPrevious player has T9 in history \u2014 they played T9 twice \u2014 so they had at least two, maybe forming a pung?  \nTheir melds show no T9 \u2014 but they could be saving for a pair or pung.  \nThey discarded T9 \u2192 so they are not keeping it \u2014 likely not collecting.  \n\nSimilarly, next player discarded T9 \u2192 not collecting.  \n\nSo T9 is safe.  \n\nBut B5:  \n- You have two chows using B5 \u2014 opponents may think you're building around B5  \n- Discarding B5 might signal you're abandoning that \u2014 but it\u2019s still a harmless middle tile  \n\nUltimately, **both are safe**, but **T9 is a terminal**, so marginally more dangerous.  \n\nTherefore, **discard T9** to reduce terminal exposure.  \n\nEvaluation: Both T9 and B5 are singleton